1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,367
By the end of this video, you will know how to measure your feet, pick the right short track boot size, and heat mould the boot so it fits your foot.

2
00:00:09,367 --> 00:00:13,957
You will also know how to check the mould over your first three sessions.

3
00:00:13,957 --> 00:00:17,226
A high end short track boot only works when it fits.

4
00:00:17,226 --> 00:00:20,181
Get it right and the boot disappears under you.

5
00:00:20,181 --> 00:00:27,222
Get it wrong and you spend your season fighting lace bite, heel lift and numb toes instead of chasing lap times.

6
00:00:27,222 --> 00:00:30,114
Fit in this sport is a craft, not a shoe size.

7
00:00:30,814 --> 00:00:34,180
A short track boot is not a stiffer fitness skate.

8
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:35,729
It is a different tool.

9
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:41,249
Short track means deep lean angles, hard crossovers and corners that never let go.

10
00:00:41,249 --> 00:00:47,779
The boot has to hold your ankle in one position and pass a sideways push straight into the blade.

11
00:00:47,779 --> 00:00:54,107
The EHS boot does this with a carbon fibre shell that refuses to twist when you load a corner.

12
00:00:54,107 --> 00:01:00,974
It is also built on a last made for short track, which supports your ankle and keeps your heel stable.

13
00:01:00,974 --> 00:01:02,522
That heel matters most.

14
00:01:02,522 --> 00:01:05,350
If the heel moves, your push leaks energy.

15
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:07,975
Running shoe logic does not apply here.

16
00:01:08,676 --> 00:01:12,946
Measure at the end of the day, when your feet are at their largest.

17
00:01:12,946 --> 00:01:15,113
Stand up, with weight on the foot.

18
00:01:15,113 --> 00:01:22,378
Put a sheet of paper against a wall, stand with your heel touching the wall, and mark the tip of your longest toe.

19
00:01:22,378 --> 00:01:25,692
That distance in millimetres is your working number.

20
00:01:25,692 --> 00:01:26,839
Measure both feet.

21
00:01:26,839 --> 00:01:28,433
Work from the larger one.

22
00:01:28,433 --> 00:01:33,085
Then measure the width across the ball of your foot, at its widest point.

23
00:01:33,085 --> 00:01:35,507
Width is where most fit problems live.

24
00:01:35,507 --> 00:01:42,709
A broad forefoot in a boot sized only on length will feel pressure on the outside of the foot within ten minutes.

25
00:01:42,709 --> 00:01:47,489
A narrow foot will find the heel floating, however hard you pull the laces.

26
00:01:48,189 --> 00:01:49,482
So how snug is snug?

27
00:01:49,482 --> 00:01:51,745
This diagram shows the three zones.

28
00:01:51,745 --> 00:01:53,815
At the back, the heel is locked.

29
00:01:53,815 --> 00:01:56,337
Across the middle, the midfoot is firm.

30
00:01:56,337 --> 00:02:04,485
At the front, your toes just brush the end when you stand upright, and pull back off it when you bend into a skating position.

31
00:02:04,485 --> 00:02:07,007
There should be no gap around the heel.

32
00:02:07,007 --> 00:02:15,284
If you can lift your heel more than a couple of millimetres with the boot laced, the boot is too big or the wrong shape for you.

33
00:02:15,284 --> 00:02:21,621
Many skaters buy half a size too large, because a properly fitted boot feels alarming in the shop.

34
00:02:21,621 --> 00:02:22,397
Resist that.

35
00:02:22,397 --> 00:02:28,346
A boot with room in it feels great for a week, then costs you a whole season of clean edges.

36
00:02:29,046 --> 00:02:36,889
If you are between two sizes, take the smaller one, as long as your toes are not jammed against the front while standing.

37
00:02:36,889 --> 00:02:39,741
A slightly snug boot settles after moulding.

38
00:02:39,741 --> 00:02:42,398
A slightly large boot never gets smaller.

39
00:02:42,398 --> 00:02:45,056
The same rule applies to growing juniors.

40
00:02:45,056 --> 00:02:48,231
A boot bought a size large teaches bad technique.

41
00:02:48,231 --> 00:02:55,620
The skater grips with the toes to make up for the loose heel, and the foot is never really connected to the blade.

42
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:00,870
Taking the smaller size and accepting a shorter service life is the better trade.

43
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:08,907
And if your numbers do not sit neatly in one size, send your measurements to the shop before you order rather than guessing.

44
00:03:09,608 --> 00:03:13,003
Heat moulding turns a very good boot into your boot.

45
00:03:13,003 --> 00:03:19,859
The carbon shell can be warmed until it softens, laced onto your foot, then left to cool into your shape.

46
00:03:19,859 --> 00:03:21,426
Here are the four steps.

47
00:03:21,426 --> 00:03:22,732
One, warm the shell.

48
00:03:22,732 --> 00:03:24,560
Two, lace it onto your foot.

49
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:34,355
Three, hold a skating posture with the knee driven forward, because moulding while standing relaxed gives you a boot shaped for standing, not skating.

50
00:03:34,355 --> 00:03:37,424
Four, let it cool fully before you take it off.

51
00:03:37,424 --> 00:03:39,579
Wear the exact socks you race in.

52
00:03:39,579 --> 00:03:43,235
And do not over tighten the laces while the shell cools.

53
00:03:43,235 --> 00:03:49,765
You want it to take your shape, not to be crushed into a shape that only works at full lace tension.

54
00:03:50,465 --> 00:03:55,194
If you have never moulded a carbon boot, have someone experienced do it.

55
00:03:55,194 --> 00:04:00,383
Overheating a carbon shell can damage the resin for good, and there is no undo.

56
00:04:00,383 --> 00:04:11,352
A good fitter controls the temperature and the time, gets the boot onto your foot quickly, holds your foot in a skating position while it cools, and checks the result.

57
00:04:11,352 --> 00:04:16,278
For temperature and time, follow the instructions that come with your boot.

58
00:04:16,278 --> 00:04:19,365
Do not guess from a video of a different brand.

59
00:04:19,365 --> 00:04:22,452
Hair dryers and home ovens are hard to control.

60
00:04:22,452 --> 00:04:29,283
A second mould is possible if the first is not right, but every heat cycle is a small cost to the shell.

61
00:04:29,283 --> 00:04:31,713
So treat the first attempt seriously.

62
00:04:32,413 --> 00:04:35,860
This is the most important idea in the whole guide.

63
00:04:35,860 --> 00:04:38,699
Moulding is a shape tool, not a size tool.

64
00:04:38,699 --> 00:04:41,403
On the left is what moulding can change.

65
00:04:41,403 --> 00:04:46,406
It can make room around an ankle bone, over a high instep, or at a bunion.

66
00:04:46,406 --> 00:04:56,613
And it can improve how closely the heel cup wraps your heel, which is the most valuable thing it does, because heel hold is where power is won or lost.

67
00:04:56,613 --> 00:04:59,587
On the right is what moulding cannot change.

68
00:04:59,587 --> 00:05:02,426
It cannot make the boot longer or shorter.

69
00:05:02,426 --> 00:05:05,333
It cannot shrink a shell that is too roomy.

70
00:05:05,333 --> 00:05:09,051
It cannot add a full width grade or fix the wrong last.

71
00:05:09,051 --> 00:05:10,809
Those are sizing problems.

72
00:05:10,809 --> 00:05:16,825
If the length or the basic shape is wrong, the answer is a different boot, not more heat.

73
00:05:17,525 --> 00:05:21,330
A mould is not finished when the boot comes off your foot.

74
00:05:21,330 --> 00:05:25,070
It is finished when three sessions on the ice confirm it.

75
00:05:25,070 --> 00:05:26,448
First, check at home.

76
00:05:26,448 --> 00:05:27,629
Lace up and stand.

77
00:05:27,629 --> 00:05:33,140
Your heel should feel pinned, with no lift when you rise onto the ball of your foot.

78
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:35,042
Drop into a skating position.

79
00:05:35,042 --> 00:05:40,816
Your toes should pull back slightly, and nothing should bite across the top of the foot.

80
00:05:40,816 --> 00:05:42,063
Then go to the ice.

81
00:05:42,063 --> 00:05:44,818
In session one, skate only twenty minutes.

82
00:05:44,818 --> 00:05:48,033
Look for sharp local pain, not general stiffness.

83
00:05:48,033 --> 00:05:51,970
In session two, note where and when any discomfort shows up.

84
00:05:51,970 --> 00:05:55,316
In session three, push properly and judge the heel.

85
00:05:55,316 --> 00:06:01,614
If it lifts on a hard push, or your foot slides forward in the corner, that is a volume problem.

86
00:06:02,314 --> 00:06:05,867
Most complaints after a mould fall into four patterns.

87
00:06:05,867 --> 00:06:10,144
One sharp spot usually means a bony area the shell did not clear.

88
00:06:10,144 --> 00:06:15,276
The fix is local relief with controlled heat on that spot, not a full remould.

89
00:06:15,276 --> 00:06:18,500
Heel lifting means too much volume for your foot.

90
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:21,461
Check your lacing first, then check the size.

91
00:06:21,461 --> 00:06:26,395
Pain across the top of the foot comes from lace pressure at the flex point.

92
00:06:26,395 --> 00:06:30,540
Relax the laces at the top and keep them firm over the midfoot.

93
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:36,462
Numb toes mean compression, often from a shell that cooled with the laces pulled too hard.

94
00:06:36,462 --> 00:06:39,686
This is the one symptom you act on straight away.

95
00:06:39,686 --> 00:06:41,265
Loosen the lacing first.

96
00:06:41,265 --> 00:06:46,726
If that does not solve it, stop and have the boot checked before your next session.

97
00:06:47,426 --> 00:06:53,969
A well moulded carbon boot does not need a long, painful break in, but it does need a sensible start.

98
00:06:53,969 --> 00:06:57,597
Skate a few shorter sessions before a full interval set.

99
00:06:57,597 --> 00:07:01,096
If the boot feels tight, do not reach for thick socks.

100
00:07:01,096 --> 00:07:05,307
They fill the space the shell was moulded to leave for your foot.

101
00:07:05,307 --> 00:07:09,000
Mould in your race socks, then skate in those same socks.

102
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,174
Remember that a short track skate is three parts.

103
00:07:12,174 --> 00:07:16,774
The boot bolts to a pair of mounting cups, and the cups hold the blade.

104
00:07:16,774 --> 00:07:20,207
The cups are a separate purchase, so budget for them.

105
00:07:20,207 --> 00:07:26,038
And dry your boots properly after every session, so the fit lasts for seasons, not months.

106
00:07:26,738 --> 00:07:28,454
Here is the short version.

107
00:07:28,454 --> 00:07:30,237
Measure your feet properly.

108
00:07:30,237 --> 00:07:31,491
Take the snug size.

109
00:07:31,491 --> 00:07:34,395
Get the boot moulded by someone experienced.

110
00:07:34,395 --> 00:07:37,366
Remember that moulding fixes shape, not size.

111
00:07:37,366 --> 00:07:43,439
Then check the mould over your first three sessions, while small corrections are still easy.

112
00:07:43,439 --> 00:07:49,974
Do those things and the boot will feel like part of your leg rather than something you are wearing.

113
00:07:49,974 --> 00:07:57,565
The full guide on the LiveLica blog covers lacing, mounting cups, blade choice and a long list of common questions.

114
00:07:57,565 --> 00:07:59,612
The link is in the description.
